ad.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use folktale with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify pattern and explain why it matters.","I can practise culture independently on a new prompt.","I can notice published needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources named in the book.","options":["Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about folktale with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA published folktale may show a clever smaller character outwitting a bully. We note the country/culture named by the author or collector and avoid inventing \"traditional\" claims we cannot source.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFrom a published folktale you know, name one pattern (trick, journey, reward) and the source book if possible.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnswer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition.","explain":"Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources named in the book.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Folktale pattern with a published source note.","caption":"Study published versions respectfully.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rec